#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 18.001pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(135.84pt, 100.68pt, "[Invited] Advances in All-silicon Waveguides  ")
  #line-at(209.88pt, 129.24pt, "for Terahertz Integration ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(172.08pt, 165.72pt, "Nguyen H. NGO, Weijie GAO, and Masayuki FUJITA ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(156.84pt, 199.32pt, "Graduate School of Engineering Science, The University of Osaka ")
  #line-at(165.84pt, 215.4pt, "1-3 Machikaneyama, Toyonaka-shi, Osaka, 560-8531 Japan ")
  #line-at(214.92pt, 234.96pt, "E-mail: ngo.h-nguyen.es@osaka-u.ac.jp ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(42.6pt, 267.6pt, "Abstract ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(83.52pt, 267.6pt, "In chip-to-chip communication, terahertz waves provide a promising approach to achieve high data capacity with ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 280.08pt, "improved energy efficiency, effectively bridging the gap between electrical and optical domains. By realizing this potential, all-")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 292.56pt, "dielectric waveguides have emerged as high-speed interconnects, offering broad bandwidth and low-loss characteristics while ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 305.04pt, "being compatible with planar integration. This paper reviews different types of terahertz all-dielectric waveguides, including ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 317.52pt, "photonic-crystal and effective-medium-clad structures, and highlights recent progress in implementing these platforms to ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 330pt, "develop various passive functional devices, paving the way for high-speed operation and large-scale integration in terahertz ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 342.6pt, "systems.   ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(42.6pt, 370.56pt, "Keywords: ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(91.44pt, 370.56pt, "Terahertz, dielectric waveguide, photonic crystal, effective medium, slot waveguide, substrateless, integrated system")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(42.6pt, 401.88pt, "1.")
  #line-at(53.88pt, 401.88pt, "Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(51.6pt, 417.12pt, "Terahertz (THz) waves, extending from 0.1 to 10 THz, have ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 427.44pt, "attracted growing interest for next-generation communication ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 437.88pt, "networks, as well as for imaging and sensing applications. THz ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 448.2pt, "communications offer enormously available bandwidth, enabling ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 458.52pt, "ultra-high data rates beyond those of current millimeter-wave ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 468.84pt, "systems. Recent demonstrations have achieved over 100 Gbit/s with ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 479.28pt, "a single channel at the 300-GHz band and 1 Tbit/s with multiple ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 489.6pt, "carrier frequencies in the WR-1.5 band (500–750 GHz) [1], ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 499.92pt, "underscoring the potential of THz links for future 6G networks. ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 510.24pt, "Besides, THz waves interact and penetrate dielectric materials, ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 520.68pt, "which ")
  #line-at(75.091pt, 520.68pt, "opens ")
  #line-at(106.447pt, 520.68pt, "unique ")
  #line-at(141.403pt, 520.68pt, "opportunities ")
  #line-at(199.424pt, 520.68pt, "in ")
  #line-at(216.885pt, 520.68pt, "spectroscopy ")
  #line-at(274.177pt, 520.68pt, "and ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 531pt, "nondestructive sensing and imaging [2]. To fully exploit the THz ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 541.32pt, "band, compact and efficient THz sources are essential. Optical-based ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 551.64pt, "generation usually requires complex system integration and low DC-")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 562.08pt, "to-RF efficiency. Conversely, electronics-based oscillators have ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 572.4pt, "higher energy efficiency and larger power density, yet fundamental ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 582.72pt, "oscillation frequencies are limited to ~2 THz with significantly ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 593.04pt, "reduced output powers [3]. Therefore, preserving power is crucial ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 603.48pt, "for high-speed inter-/intra-chip communication systems, enabling ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 613.8pt, "the development of low-loss THz interconnects. ")
  #line-at(51.6pt, 627.12pt, "Another challenge in conventional THz systems is the high ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 637.44pt, "propagation loss of metallic-based waveguides at these frequencies. ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 647.88pt, "Traditional microwave-inspired guiding structures (e.g., metallic ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 658.2pt, "rectangular waveguides or coaxial cable) suffer severe ohmic losses ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 668.52pt, "in the THz regime. Although metallic hollow waveguides can ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 678.84pt, "achieve low attenuation (theoretically ~2 dB/cm up to 1 THz [4]) ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 689.28pt, "with most waves propagating in the air core, they are not applicable ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 699.6pt, "to build integrated circuits for their non-planar geometry. THz ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 709.92pt, "coplanar waveguides are suitable for planar integration; however, ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 720.24pt, "metal-dependent structures also exhibit larger attenuation (e.g., ~35 ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 730.68pt, "dB/cm at 750 GHz [5]) compared to metallic hollow waveguides. To ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 741pt, "eliminate the ohmic loss, dielectric waveguides (e.g., silicon-on-")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 751.32pt, "insulator or all-silicon waveguides) have emerged for efficient THz ")
  #line-at(42.6pt, 761.64pt, "signal transmission, where high-resistivity silicon waveguides have ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 399.96pt, "demonstrated ~2 order-of-magnitude lower attenuation compared to ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 410.4pt, "their metallic counterparts at 1 THz [6].   ")
  #line-at(317.28pt, 423.72pt, "Planar waveguides are therefore considered as a candidate for THz ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 434.04pt, "integrated systems. A simple high-index ribbon waveguide ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 444.36pt, "surrounded by air confines waves within the core through total ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 454.8pt, "internal reflection (TIR). By introducing effective-medium or ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 465.12pt, "photonic crystal cladding, a substrate-less platform is realized for ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 475.44pt, "multiple purposes, including the ease of waveguide handling. ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 485.76pt, "Notably, ")
  #line-at(348.942pt, 485.76pt, "this ")
  #line-at(371.874pt, 485.76pt, "substrate-less ")
  #line-at(430.1pt, 485.76pt, "platform ")
  #line-at(471.157pt, 485.76pt, "is ")
  #line-at(486.979pt, 485.76pt, "compatible ")
  #line-at(536.906pt, 485.76pt, "with ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 496.2pt, "microfabrication processes, such as deep reactive-ion etching, ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 506.52pt, "thereby lowering fabrication complexity and eliminating the excess ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 516.84pt, "loss inherent in platforms like silicon-on-glass. In addition to low ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 527.16pt, "loss, all-dielectric waveguides can offer broad operational ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 537.48pt, "bandwidth with low dispersion, which is critical for high-speed data ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 547.92pt, "transmission. ")
  #line-at(365.554pt, 547.92pt, "Besides, ")
  #line-at(405.198pt, 547.92pt, "all-dielectric ")
  #line-at(459.96pt, 547.92pt, "waveguides provide a ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 558.24pt, "foundation for various passive components (e.g., filters, couplers, ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 568.56pt, "antennas), highlighting their potential for practical THz front-end ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 578.88pt, "devices. In this paper, we present the fundamentals of all-dielectric ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 589.32pt, "planar waveguides, being categorized based on their guiding ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 599.64pt, "mechanism, and review our recent advances in THz integrated ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 609.96pt, "components developed from these waveguides.  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(308.28pt, 634.2pt, "2.")
  #line-at(319.56pt, 634.2pt, "Fundamentals of all-dielectric planar waveguides ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(317.28pt, 649.44pt, "Dielectric waveguides confine THz waves through TIR or ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 659.88pt, "photonic bandgap (PBG) effects. They serve as the fundamental ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 670.2pt, "building blocks for passive components such as splitters, resonators, ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 680.52pt, "and mode converters. Thanks to these advantages, all-dielectric ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 690.84pt, "waveguides are poised to become key building blocks for THz ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 701.28pt, "integrated circuits and systems, providing the low-loss interconnects ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 711.6pt, "and functional elements needed for practical THz communications, ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 721.92pt, "sensing, and imaging devices.  ")
  #line-at(317.28pt, 735.24pt, "Depending on the guiding mechanism and cladding geometry, ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 745.68pt, "THz dielectric waveguides can be categorized into (a) air clad, (b) ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 756pt, "photonic-crystal clad, (c) effective-medium clad, and (d) slot-based ")
  #line-at(308.28pt, 766.32pt, "waveguides, as illustrated in Fig. 1. ")
]

